summary:
Root cause, measured: players can't tell WHEN to parry because the only timing cue shows up after they could have reacted. Bad animation and the dark scenes make it worse. I replicated Enemy.TickAttack + the Snappy timing curve in Python at 60 fps (telegraph_sim.py). The blade glint (Enemy.cs:461, fires at stepNorm >= activeStart-0.18) arrives only 0.10-0.18 s before the strike for every ninja, elite, sumo, Mizuchi and Ōzeki attack (ninja Attack1 0.18 s, Attack2 0.12 s, Attack3 0.15 s; sumo slaps 0.12/0.18 s). Human visual reaction is ~0.25-0.30 s, and the parry only works if pressed in [strike-0.24 s, strike] (PlayerConfig.parryWindow 0.24, perfect 0.11). In a Monte Carlo with RT ~ N(0.27, 0.05) s, reacting to the glint succeeds 0-4% of the time on common enemies. Parry is guesswork.

The enemy swing SFX starts at the glint and peaks 0.11-0.13 s later (enemy_swing_*.wav), i.e. on the hit itself. It is an impact sound, not a warning.

The "telegraph" hold is mostly lost. holdEnd (Enemy.cs:448) assumes the clip runs at speed 1, but the wind-up runs at 0.85. Ninja Attack1's 0.12 s hold becomes 0.03 s and Gorō Combo1's 0.20 s becomes 0.10 s. The hold also sits at activeStart-0.12, which is already mid-swing, not at the cocked pose.

Gorō's damage frames do not match his clips. I measured the hammer-head speed in Blender (Minijefe.fbx, Cylinder.005 on bone BaseM):
- Combo1: the swing is at n=0.64-0.83 but activeStart is 0.48, so damage lands ~0.19 s before the hammer moves.
- Combo3: swing at 0.62-0.73 vs 0.45, ~0.15 s early.
- Heavy slam: hits the ground at 0.73-0.78 vs 0.55, so the shockwave fires ~0.65 s before the hammer lands.
- Combo2 matches (0.51-0.54 vs 0.50).

The sumo's attack clips barely move. Silhouette change across the contact sheets: Attack1 16%, Attack2 8%, Special 10%, Idle and Exhausted 0%, versus 90% for Gorō Combo1. Its wind-ups are literally invisible.

Bug: the common sumo's unblockable charge ("Embestida imparable") never moves. Charge is only implemented in Boss.TickSpecial. A regular sumo stands still 3.5-11 m away, but still fires the danger flash and the 危 marker, and can trigger the one-time dash tutorial slow-mo for an attack that cannot connect.

Visibility. In combat, a ninja is 113-134 px tall at 1080p (camera model, confirmed ≈137 px in finisher_prompt2.png). The katana is about 3 px thick, dark blue, on near-black bodies (BlackFloor 0.13) at night. In finisher_prompt.png only the HP bars of three ninjas are visible. The glint is a 0.09 s white sprite. On the lake arena it is camouflaged by white/cyan water sparkles.

Unblockables give no countdown. The danger cue fires 0.65-1.88 s before the hit, but dash i-frames last only 0.22 s, so players dash too early. Simulated dodge success: Gorō slam 7%, spin 0%.

Kanji: 危 / 防 / 処刑 are still used in combat markers and in the dash tutorial text.

Camera. The pitch (45-47°) is fine. Ground telegraphs read best from a high camera. The problems are distance and framing: boss framing reaches 31 m (Kaito 66 px), and long-range attacks can start off-screen with no indicator.

Parry feedback darkens the scene. Perfect-parry slow-mo makes ScreenFX desaturate by ~31 (ScreenFX.cs:126), against the design doc's "parry lights up the scene". The ParryStance clip is a near-static 0.10 s, and a whiffed parry gives no feedback, so players can't learn whether they were early or late.

Proposed system: an honest, time-based "ensō" tell. A brush-stroke circle is drawn on the ground under the attacker from the moment the attack starts. It closes 0.06 s before a parryable strike, the middle of the perfect window. It is gold over a dark ink underlay so it reads on night ground, snow and water. Unblockables get a crimson serrated ring plus the real hit area, closing 0.12 s before the strike to centre the dash i-frames. Supporting cues:
- minimum wind-up times enforced by a deterministic step timeline (exact StrikeEta);
- the weapon glows and a point light builds on the attacker;
- procedural anticipation lean/crouch, needed now because of the static sumo clips;
- audio: a blade "shing" or taiko hit at onset, plus a hyōshigi wood-clack 0.30 s before the strike;
- a strike scheduler keeping different attackers' hits ≥ 0.42 s apart;
- parry/dash allowed at any point of Kaito's swing except the active frames;
- a parry-window crescent and early/late feedback;
- a parry that brightens the scene (exposure and bloom spike, no desaturation);
- combat camera 15-23 m (was 18-28), off-screen threat chevrons, and no kanji.

In the same Monte Carlo, the proposed tells give 93-100% parry/dodge success for a player who has learned the cue (65-85% perfect), up from 0-35% today. Difficulty should come from mixups that the ring still shows truthfully, not from unreadable timing. Mock-ups at true in-game scale and perspective, the lead-time chart, measurement scripts and placeholder audio are in the scratch folder.
